  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brandywine Bay began as a swampy tract that once hosted rattlesnakes, copperheads, and water moccasins before development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brandywineowners.org/about/history/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community grew from a 1970s golf‑course vision by Jim Gardner and Ed Rawls, who saw opportunity in the wooded, swampy land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brandywineowners.org/about/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before Brandywine’s homes appeared, the area was a bustling tobacco, corn, and peanut farm run by Gerald Murdoch’s father on the north side of Highway 70.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brandywineowners.org/about/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the 1930s, Gerald Murdoch recalled hunting squirrels in Brandywine’s woods, a pastime that faded as the area transformed into a residential community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brandywineowners.org/about/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Brandywine Owners Association now oversees 639 homes and lots on the north side of Highway 70, a legacy of the original gated community plan.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brandywineowners.org/about/history/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Brandywine Bay sits in west‑central Carteret County, just west of Morehead City, and its growth mirrors the broader rise of the Crystal Coast as a retirement and tourist hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
